lines=['# Compatibility null validation','',f"Protocol SHA-256: `{m.check_lock()['protocol_sha256']}`.",'','All eight frozen Table2 raw means and sample SDs reproduced. Strict d>0 and d>1e-6 agreed for those predictions.','', '## Complete result tables','', '- results_table.csv: requested setting, fitting/evaluation engine counts, rate, distance and 95% engine-bootstrap CIs. Rates are fractions, distances are in probability units.','- synthetic_summary.csv: cohort means and conditional CIs, plus training-replication ranges/percentiles and oracle-error diagnostics.','- synthetic_per_replication.csv: every replication and its engine-bootstrap CIs.','- real_vs_synthetic.csv: every prespecified groupwise comparison.','- synthetic_oracle_noise.csv: oracle sanity checks and all noisy-quantile levels.','- auxiliary_cuda_results.csv and auxiliary_cuda_inventory.json: every auxiliary CUDA result and the interrupted state.','', '## Counts and uncertainty','', 'Temporal: 30 independent fitting fleets per 8 configurations, two separately trained objectives (480 fits). Covariate: 200 per 3 fitting sizes/objectives (1200 CPU fits). Parametric: 200 fitting fleets per 3 sizes / 2 endpoint policies (1200 fitted control configurations; each has independently estimated quantile and event functions).','', 'Fitting engines: FD001 60, FD002 156, FD003 60, FD004 149. Evaluation engines: small 100/259/100/248; large 10,000 per subset/policy. Each small fleet is a prefix of its large fleet. Evaluation engine IDs recur across fits and variants; model/seed counts never multiply the engine count.','', 'The 95% percentile engine-bootstrap CIs condition on the complete fitted cohort. They do not include fitting uncertainty. The separately reported replication spread varies independent fitting fleets and is not a bootstrap over seeds.','', '## Groupwise exceedance of the locked synthetic benchmark','', 'The locked descriptive criterion compares each reproduced real point estimate with the 97.5th percentile of independent fitting-replication rates on the 10,000-engine final-window synthetic fleet. This is not a universal null test or a significance statement.','']
for family in ['temporal','covariate','parametric']:
 for variant in ['weighted_raw','weighted_corrected','unweighted']:
    g=[r for r in contrasts if r['family']==family and r['variant']==variant]
    n=sum(r['exceeds_prespecified_benchmark']=='True' for r in g)
    lines.append(f'- {family}, {variant}: {n}/{len(g)} real subset/architecture results exceed the specified synthetic benchmark. See every group and engine count in real_vs_synthetic.csv.')
lines+=['','Only weighted_raw null comparisons share the raw Table2 event-output convention. Corrected and unweighted comparisons against the raw real rate are descriptive cross-variant contrasts, not matched real-data tests. Neural epoch/LR/batch schedules match the inherited budget; synthetic lifetime lengths produce different window and optimizer-step counts, which are retained in training_integrity_checks.csv.','', 'The counts describe the prespecified synthetic settings; they do not establish a universal finite-sample null for C-MAPSS.','', 'The real Table2 statistic averages five initializations on one fitting fleet. Each synthetic replication has a fresh fitting fleet with one initialization per objective. The upper-tail comparison is therefore a descriptive benchmark, not a matched five-initialization null test.','', '## Scope and numerical findings','', 'Temporal and covariate neural experiments are known-law training-pipeline nulls including approximation, optimization, weighting and endpoint-sampling effects. They are not pure estimation-noise measurements. Parametric functions include the known conditional law, but their endpoint-only fitting data have a different information budget from the neural full-window fitting data; the differences do not isolate a causal approximation-error component.','', 'Weibull shape 1 is the exponential special case. Final-window ages reproduce empirical input-file truncation-length marginals; NASA\'s latent censoring mechanism is not identified. Uniform complete-lifetime endpoints have a different analytic conditional law (E1 exponential integral). Oracle quantiles are computed analytically or by deterministic inversion, never from a trained model.','', 'Final-policy saturated parametric quantile and event functions are independently fitted loss minimizers on the same data. Their exact solutions are compatible despite finite-sample error. Exact integer arithmetic verified this; one floating-point strict violation has maximum distance 5.551115123125783e-17. Strict results remain in tables alongside the 1e-6 sensitivity; this numerical artifact is not estimation noise.','', 'True-quantile/true-probability sanity checks have zero incompatibility and zero distance for every fleet/policy. This is an implementation identity, not evidence of predictive calibration.','', 'Training integrity checks verify budgets, units, class weights, source identities and parameter counts. Selected saved full-fleet forwards were independently replayed on CPU. Convergence histories and oracle errors remain reported; fixed budgets were not extended after inspecting outcomes.','', 'The covariate compute-only CPU amendment was based on random-input timing. The 11 completed auxiliary CUDA fits and interrupted state are retained separately; no performance criterion selected the CPU cohort.','', '## AI provenance and review','', 'Configured assistant model: gpt-6.1-sol, medium reasoning, from current-chat metadata. Exposed Codex runtime: 0.166.0-alpha.5.1. Exact served checkpoint/build is not exposed. Reference truth came from the declared law; predictions came from fitted research models. The assistant authored and executed code. See ai_provenance.json/session_provenance.json and build_provenance.json (desktop app 26.1027.3384.0 and runtime executable SHA256).','', 'External independent review: REVIEW_UNAVAILABLE. Executor checks are not an independent scientific audit. All commands/configurations and the retained initial collector failure are logged.']
investigation=json.loads((m.OUT/'extreme_rate_investigation.json').read_text())
probes=read('lower_head_gradient_probes.csv')
section=['## Investigation of extreme temporal rates','',f"Across all 480 temporal fits, {investigation['fits_with_gap_below_001_on_over99percent_engines']} fitted states had q50-q05 below 0.001 cycles on more than 99% of their 10,000 evaluation engines. Fitting counts were 60/156/60/149 engines for FD001-FD004. This is a descriptive count of fitted states, without a model-level independence assumption.",'','Some extreme rates coincide with inaccurate lower quantiles, sometimes almost equal to the median. All original fits remain in the tables. The fixed post-hoc probes used FD001 replication 0, 60 fitting engines and all 4,209 fitting windows per model; gradients were measured without an optimizer step.','']
for r in probes:
    section.append(f"- {r['architecture']}, weighted={r['weighted']}, fitting engines 60: mean softplus derivative {float(r['softplus_derivative_mean']):.4g}; lower-head gradient norm {float(r['lower_delta_head_gradient_norm']):.4g}; median-head norm {float(r['median_head_gradient_norm']):.4g}.")
section+=['','The two hybrid probes demonstrate lower-offset saturation at those checkpoints. The LSTM probes differ, so a single explanation for every extreme fit is not established. No longer-budget convergence-control refit was run. These results cannot be attributed purely to estimation noise; the no-exceedance counts describe the locked pipelines and controls, and do not establish that estimation noise explains the real-data incompatibility. See temporal_extreme_rate_investigation.csv, temporal_lower_gap_all_fits.csv, lower_head_gradient_probes.csv and investigation_protocol.md.','']
position=lines.index('## Groupwise exceedance of the locked synthetic benchmark')
lines[position:position]=section
(m.OUT/'report.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines),encoding='utf-8')
import matplotlib
matplotlib.use('Agg')
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
per=read('synthetic_per_replication.csv');fig,axes=plt.subplots(2,4,figsize=(16,8),sharey=True)
variants=['weighted_raw','weighted_corrected','unweighted']
for ax,(arch,sub) in zip(axes.flat,[(a,s) for a in ['lstm','hybrid'] for s in m.SUBS]):
    data=[]
    for v in variants:data.append([100*float(r['incompatibility_rate']) for r in per if r['family']=='temporal' and r['subset']==sub and r['architecture']==arch and r['policy']=='final' and r['variant']==v and r['evaluation_engines']=='10000'])
    ax.boxplot(data,tick_labels=['Weighted','Corrected','Unweighted'],showfliers=True)
    real=next(r for r in gate['summary'] if r['subset']==sub and r['model']==('LSTM' if arch=='lstm' else 'Hybrid'))
    ax.axhline(real['recomputed_mean'],color='red',linestyle='--',label=f"Real n={real['engines']}")
    ax.set_title(f"{sub} {arch}; fit n={m.CFG['fit_counts'][sub]}");ax.set_ylabel('Incompatible (%)');ax.tick_params(axis='x',rotation=20);ax.legend(fontsize=8)
fig.suptitle('30 fitting-fleet replications; synthetic evaluation n=10,000 engines\nBoxes show training-fleet spread, not engine-bootstrap confidence intervals')
fig.tight_layout();fig.savefig(m.OUT/'temporal_null_comparison.png',dpi=180);fig.savefig(m.OUT/'temporal_null_comparison.pdf');plt.close(fig)
